From: haroldxie <xiewenhao2308@icloud.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 14:39:50 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] arm64: fpsimd: export fpsimd_restore_current_state() for the
 KJIT runtime

The KJIT runtime runs verified translations of a task's user code at EL1
on the syscall return path, before the task goes back to userspace. Some
of that code (glibc memcpy, memset, strlen) uses FP/SIMD registers, and
the translation keeps V0-V31, FPCR and FPSR as the user's registers,
live in hardware, exactly as userspace would see them.

That is only correct when the CPU's FP/SIMD registers hold current's
user state, i.e. TIF_FOREIGN_FPSTATE is clear. During a syscall it may
be set: the task was scheduled out, or kernel-mode NEON (a syscall or a
softirq) took the registers. The normal return to userspace then calls
fpsimd_restore_current_state() from arch_exit_to_user_mode_work(). The
runtime needs the same reload earlier, right before it runs such code:

	local_bh_disable();	/* no softirq NEON, no preemption (!RT) */
	if (test_thread_flag(TIF_FOREIGN_FPSTATE))
		fpsimd_restore_current_state();
	pagefault_disable();
	/* run the translated user code */
	pagefault_enable();
	local_bh_enable();

After fpsimd_restore_current_state() the registers are bound to the task
(TIF_FOREIGN_FPSTATE clear), so a later context switch or softirq NEON
saves what the translated code wrote, and the exit path has nothing to
reload. The function already handles every FP/SIMD configuration and its
own locking (get_cpu_fpsimd_context() nests inside the runtime's
local_bh_disable()), so export it GPL-only instead of duplicating any of
it in the module. Nothing else changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c
index 60a45d600..bed3fca1e 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c
@@ -1804,6 +1804,7 @@ void fpsimd_restore_current_state(void)
 
 	put_cpu_fpsimd_context();
 }
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(fpsimd_restore_current_state);
 
 void fpsimd_update_current_state(struct user_fpsimd_state const *state)
 {
